\details{
A pamDP package holds three tables: \code{deployments.csv} (one row per
recorder deployment, with its location), \code{media.csv} (one row per audio
file) and \code{observations.csv} (one row per classified event, with the
offsets \code{eventStart} and \code{eventEnd} in seconds from the start of the
audio file). When the folder has a \code{datapackage.json}, the paths of the
three tables are taken from its resources (\code{deployments}, \code{media},
\code{observations}); otherwise the files are looked for by those names.

\strong{Predictions.} Observations of a classifier are kept: those with
\code{classificationMethod} equal to \code{"machine"} (or empty) and a
\code{classificationProbability}, of type \code{"animal"} when \code{observationType} is
given, with a \code{scientificName} and a \code{mediaID} found in \code{media.csv}.
Observations classified by people (\code{classificationMethod = "human"}) are
left out with a message: pamDP has no field that links a human
classification to the prediction it checks, so they cannot be read as
verdicts. The columns are mapped as follows:\tabular{ll}{
   pamDP \tab sheepdog \cr
   \code{classificationProbability} \tab \code{confidence} \cr
   \code{eventStart}, \code{eventEnd} \tab \code{begin_time}, \code{end_time}; \code{file_offset} = \code{eventStart} \cr
   \code{filePath} of the media \tab \code{begin_path} \cr
   \code{frequencyLow}, \code{frequencyHigh} \tab \code{low_freq}, \code{high_freq} \cr
   \code{scientificName} \tab \code{species_code}, see below \cr
   \code{site_column} of the deployment \tab \code{site} \cr
}


sheepdog names the folder of a species after the part of \code{species_code}
after the last underscore, as in BirdNET labels
(\code{"Adenomera diptyx_ade-dip"} gives \code{ade_dip}). The scientific name
alone has no such part, so \code{species_code} is built as the scientific name
and its lower-case, hyphenated form: \code{"Boana raniceps"} becomes
\code{"Boana raniceps_boana-raniceps"}, whose folder is \code{boana_raniceps}.

\strong{Recordings.} Every row of \code{media.csv} is a recording, including the
ones without any prediction, which the occasions of the occupancy models
and \code{"random_recordings"} need. The table has \code{site} and \code{source_audio}
(the same path as \code{begin_path}), as \code{\link[=list_recordings]{list_recordings()}} does, plus
\code{media_id}, \code{deployment_id}, \code{timestamp} and \code{file_length} (seconds).

\strong{Dates and hours.} sheepdog takes the date and hour of a recording from
its file name (\code{YYYYMMDD_HHMMSS}), not from the \code{timestamp} of
\code{media.csv}. The files whose names do not carry them are reported;
rename them, or the nights and hours of the occasions will be missing.
